### 📈 **Outcomes & Learnings – WayFinder Agent**

#### ✅ Success Criteria

We looked at a few key things to measure if the agent was working well:

* How many questions were answered directly by the agent (without needing a human)
* How accurate or helpful the answers were
* If people were able to solve their issues faster
* How many users started using the self-help options after launch

These helped us understand if the WayFinder agent actually made a difference for clients and community users.

---

#### 📊 Results Summary

* Most of the traffic went to **Job #1 – Knowledge Answers**, and we saw a good number of cases getting resolved just from article suggestions.
* For **Job #3 – AR Questions**, clients really liked getting invoice status quickly without creating tickets.
* **Webinar suggestions (Job #2)** got decent clicks, especially when the webinar matched a product they recently used.
* The more complex flows like **Job #4 (Missing Remits)** had fewer users, but those who used it got guided well through the steps or logged a case quickly.

---

#### ⚠️ Challenges

* Some backend systems like AR and Finance had delay issues, so the agent didn’t always get real-time data.
* A few prompts from users were too vague (e.g., “What’s happening with my payment?”), so we had to tweak the agent to ask better follow-up questions.
* Also had to add guardrails to make sure the agent doesn’t respond to things like legal questions or unrelated topics.

---

#### 🔁 Lessons for Scaling

* Start small and test each job before rolling out to a wider audience—it helped us catch issues early.
* Get compliance and legal teams involved from the beginning so there are no surprises later.
* Have a few human reviewers monitor agent performance in the early stages to keep quality high, especially for jobs tied to finance or support tickets.

